WEBVTT

1
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.400
The channel's here on the chart.
So are the marking lights.

2
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.600
Then what's wrong with them?

3
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.200
Those lights don't seem to be
in just the right place.

4
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.700
They're both a bit out of position,
according to this.

5
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.900
Two light buoys mean a safe channel
between the world over.

6
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.200
"Safe between the world over"
doesn't go in these waters.

7
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.500
Look here.

8
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.800
You'll see the water shoals
on the island side...

9
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.100
while the deep soundings
run to the mainland.

10
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.900
Have any of you seen
the captain today?

11
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.400
No. He wasn't down for dinner.

12
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.600
No, and he wasn't
down for lunch.

13
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.500
He hasn't left the bridge since you
decided to come through the channel.

14
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.500
What are you driving at?

15
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.100
Ever since you gave him those orders
yesterday to cut through these waters...

16
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.700
he's had the jitters.

17
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.700
There's something wrong. I...

18
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.000
Hey, I'm getting nervous myself.

19
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.400
Doc, what do you recommend for nerves?

20
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.200
- Give him a shot of scotch.
- Give the whole bottle.

21
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.700
- No! I've got nerves too.
- Here you are, Doc.

22
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.800
Just what you need.

23
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.600
Well, maybe you're right.

24
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.000
And how, boy.

25
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.000
- Good evening, Captain.
- Good evening, sir.

26
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.600
- May I speak with you?
- Why, certainly.

27
00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.000
Go ahead.

28
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.000
We're heading straight for the channel
between Branca Island and the mainland.

29
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.300
Good.

30
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.500
But the lights are just a bit
off, according to the chart.

31
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.600
The charts are never up to date in this
part of the Pacific. You know that.

32
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:10.800
I know, sir, but...

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.200
Doesn't Branca Island
mean anything to you?

34
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.300
Well, not a lot.

35
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.800
Perhaps if I spoke with Mr.
Rainsford...

36
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.500
Bob's not a sailor.
He's a hunter.

37
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.400
He's made many of these trips.
He's young, but he has judgment.

38
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.100
I'll call him.

39
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.500
Oh, Bob. Bob!

40
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:28.500
What is it?

41
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.800
- Come up here, will ya?
- Just a minute.

42
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.600
What's bothering you, Captain?

43
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.100
There are no more coral-reefed,
shark-infested waters

44
00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.000
in the whole world than these.

45
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.500
Boy! Just take a look at these.

46
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.900
You didn't turn out so hot as a hunter,
Doc, but oh, what a photographer.

47
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.300
If we'd had you to take pictures
on the Sumatran trip...

48
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.800
they might have believed my book.

49
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.400
If you'd had me on the Sumatran trip,
you'd have never had me on this one.

50
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.400
Say, here's a swell one
of the ship, Skipper.

51
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.000
What's the matter?

52
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.100
These old sea dogs tell yarns
to kid each other...

53
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:00.800
and end up
believing it all themselves.

54
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.000
I think that Mr. Rainsford
should know...

55
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.600
that the channel lights aren't just
in the position given on the charts.

56
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:06.600
Oh...

57
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.700
Well, what do you think, fellas?

58
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.700
I think we should turn back
and take the outside course.

59
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.500
We'll go ahead.

60
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.200
Very well, sir.
It's your ship.

61
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.700
"It was the schooner 'Hesperus,'
and she sailed the wintry sea."

62
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.200
Now, wait a minute, fellas.
Let's talk this over.

63
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.300
There's no use
taking any chances.

64
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.400
Chances? That's fine talk...

65
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.800
coming from a fella who just got through
slapping tigers in the face.

66
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.400
Get an eyeful of this.

67
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.700
And he talks about taking chances.

68
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.200
Here's the doc charging the enemy
with an unloaded camera.

69
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.100
Get the expression
on Doc's face, Bill.

70
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.900
He looks more frightened
than the tiger.

71
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:47.900
He is.

72
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.500
What'd you have on
your mind, Doc?

73
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.500
I'll tell you
what I had on my mind.

74
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.300
I was thinking of the inconsistency
of civilization.

75
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.700
The beast of the jungle killing just
for his existence is called savage.

76
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.600
The man, killing just for sport,
is called civilized.

77
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.000
- Hear! Hear!
- It's a bit contradictory, isn't it?

78
00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.000
Now, just a minute.
What makes you think...

79
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.300
it isn't just as much sport
for the animal as it is for the man?

80
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.300
Take that fellow right there,
for instance.

81
00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.000
There never was a time
when he couldn't have gotten away.

82
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.900
He didn't want to.
He got interested in hunting me.

83
00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.400
He didn't hate me
for stalking him...

84
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.700
any more than I hated him
for trying to charge me.

85
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.200
As a matter of fact,
we admired each other.

86
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.400
Perhaps, but would you change places
with the tiger?

87
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.200
Well, not now.

88
00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.000
Here comes
that bad-luck lady again.

89
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.600
Third time tonight.

90
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.000
- Here. Let me shuffle them.
- Wait a minute. Don't evade the issue.

91
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.600
- Yeah, speak up.
- I asked you a question.

92
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.600
- You did? I forgot.
- Oh, no, you didn't.

93
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.300
I asked you if there'd be
as much sport in the game...

94
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.300
if you were the tiger
instead of the hunter.

95
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:55.900
What's your answer, Bob?

96
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.200
That's something
I'll never have to decide.

97
00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.100
Listen here, you fellows.

98
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.600
This world's divided into
two kinds of people...

99
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.200
the hunter and the hunted.

100
00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.300
Luckily, I'm a hunter.

101
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:06.900
Nothing can ever change that.

102
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.600
Hang on!

103
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.600
Hello! Hello, down there!
Hello, Engine Room!

104
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:23.800
The power board's flooded!

105
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:26.100
If the water hits
those hot boilers...

106
00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.000
Help!

107
00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.200
Help!

108
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.200
You trying to drown me?

109
00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:24.700
Where are the others?

110
00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.000
See anybody?

111
00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.000
Nobody left but us two and...

112
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:30.900
that fella.

113
00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.500
Doc! Help!

114
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.800
- Look!
- Shark!

115
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.100
Ohh! It got me!

116
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.700
Hello!
Is anybody here?

117
00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.400
Anybody around, I say.

118
00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.000
Oh, hello.

119
00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:05.300
Is this your house?

120
00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.900
I'm not trying to break in,
but I've been in a wreck.

121
00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.200
Our yacht just sunk
with all hands.

122
00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.100
I got ashore and found
your place here by accident.

123
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.000
I'm not trying to intrude,
but I'm in sort of a jam.

124
00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.200
Don't you understand
any English?

125
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.300
Ivan does not speak any language.

126
00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.000
He has the misfortune
to be dumb.

127
00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:37.300
Oh, hello.

128
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.200
Are you the owner here?

129
00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:40.200
Yes.

130
00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.800
Welcome to my poor fortress.

131
00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:44.300
Fortress?

132
00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:45.800
It once was.

133
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.700
Built by the Portuguese,
centuries ago.

134
00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:52.100
I have had the ruins restored
to make my home here.

135
00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.900
I am Count Zaroff.

136
00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.400
My name's Robert Rainsford.

137
00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:57.900
Glad to meet you.

138
00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:00.900
Very glad.

139
00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.800
Ivan is a Cossack.

140
00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:07.600
I am afraid, like all my fellow
countrymen, he is a bit of a savage.

141
00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:09.800
Smile, Ivan.

142
00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.600
I was trying to make him understand
there'd been a shipwreck in the channel.

143
00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.500
But how appalling!

144
00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:30.600
And you mean to say
that you are the only survivor?

145
00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:31.900
Yes,

146
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:33.900
I'm afraid I am.

147
00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:36.900
You're certain?

148
00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:40.600
I'd have never left the spot
if I hadn't been.

149
00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.400
The swellest crowd on Earth...

150
00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.000
my best friends.

151
00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:45.700
It's incredible.

152
00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:48.000
Such things are
always incredible.

153
00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:51.100
Death is for others,
not for ourselves.

154
00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:54.500
That is how most
of my other guests have felt.

155
00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:56.600
Your other guests?

156
00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.000
You mean this has
happened before?

157
00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:02.500
My fellow, we have several survivors
from the last wreck still in the house.

158
00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:06.200
It would seem that this island
were cursed.

159
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.500
That's just what the captain said.

160
00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:10.800
Only he thought
it was uninhabited.

161
00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:14.200
We Cossacks find our inspiration
in solitude.

162
00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:16.700
Well, it's a break
for me, anyway.

163
00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:18.400
My house is yours.

164
00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.900
Oh, by the way. You'll want to change
those wet rags immediately.

165
00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.200
Yes. They look
about the way I feel.

166
00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.400
Yes.

167
00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.500
I have some loose hunting clothes
which I keep for my guests...

168
00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.100
that you can possibly
get into.

169
00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.900
Ivan will show you
to your room.

170
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:37.100
Thank you.

171
00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:43.000
You'll find a stiff
drink there also.

172
00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:44.800
Thanks a lot.

173
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.200
All pleasure is mine.

174
00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:19.000
Come in.

175
00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:24.100
- Ready, Rainsford?
- All set.

176
00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.400
I'm afraid
we have finished dinner.

177
00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.400
But I have ordered
something for you.

178
00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:33.100
Thanks. I don't feel
like eating, though.

179
00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:34.200
Dear, dear.

180
00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:36.300
Oh. Well, perhaps later.

181
00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.500
Now, then,
what do you say to coffee...

182
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.000
and most charming company?

183
00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.700
It is hard to forget
your comrades' fate, I know...

184
00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:53.000
but our feminine guest
is easily perturbed.

185
00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:56.900
If I could beg you to put
a good face upon the matter.

186
00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:00.500
A-Assume a cheerfulness
you may not feel.

187
00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.100
- Why, sure. Of course.
- Thank you.

188
00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.300
Miss Trowbridge, may I present
Mr. Robert Rainsford.

189
00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:15.800
- Miss Eve Trowbridge.
- How do you do?

190
00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:18.700
- How do you do?
- And her brother, Mr. Martin Trowbridge.

191
00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.200
How are you, old chap?
Pretty well shaken up, I guess, huh?

192
00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.600
- Coming out of it now, thanks.
- We know how it feels,

193
00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:24.600
don't we, Eve?

194
00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:26.100
Indeed we do.

195
00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.600
Perhaps Mr. Rainsford would like
some hot coffee.

196
00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.200
Oh, yes, of course.
Mr. Rainsford, please sit here.

197
00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:33.000
Thank you

198
00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.000
Vodka, that's the stuff!

199
00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.000
One shot'll dry you out quicker
than all the coffee in Java.

200
00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.600
Have to toss it off, though.
Like this.

201
00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.300
Now, Martin, you don't have
to drink it all tonight, do you?

202
00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.900
Don't be ridiculous, sis.
We are victims of circumstance.

203
00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:50.500
Same as Mr. Rainsford.

204
00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:54.400
And if anyone has a right to his liquor,
it's a victim of circumstance.

205
00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.200
- Isn't that so, Count?
- Of course, yes.

206
00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.500
- You were in a shipwreck too?
- Yes.

207
00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:01.000
Our lifeboat
was the only one saved...

208
00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:03.000
my brother and I
and two sailors.

209
00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:06.600
The count found us on the beach with
nothing but the clothes on our backs.

210
00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.900
Those channel lights
must have been shifted.

211
00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:12.000
- I wonder it hasn't been reported.
- Well, we'll report 'em...

212
00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.100
just as soon as we get back
to the mainland.

213
00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:16.900
You see, the count
has only one launch...

214
00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.300
and that's under repair.

215
00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:24.400
Russians are not
the best mechanics.

216
00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:28.900
I'm afraid we'll have to be patient
a few days longer.

217
00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.300
It's all right with me. I feel as if I
were living on borrowed time right now.

218
00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:36.300
Speaking of that,
perhaps now you'll tell us...

219
00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:37.700
a little bit
about who you are.

220
00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:41.500
Just sketchily, you know...
born, married, why I left my last job.

221
00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:43.800
No, no, no, no.
One moment, please.

222
00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.500
Mr. Rainsford need never explain
who he is in my house.

223
00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.800
We entertain a celebrity,
Miss Trowbridge.

224
00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.300
Wait a minute, wait a minute.
Don't tell me. Let me guess.

225
00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.700
I know. Flagpole sitter.

226
00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:02.300
- I know. He wrote some books.
- No, he lived some books.

227
00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.600
If I am not mistaken,
this is Mr. Robert Rainsford...

228
00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.900
who hunts big game
so adventurously.

229
00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.400
Yeah? Here's to ya.

230
00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:12.600
- I've lugged a gun around a little.
- "I've lugged a gun around a little."

231
00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:16.400
No, I have read your books.
I read all books on hunting.

232
00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:18.500
- A papiroso?
- Thank you.

233
00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:23.700
Only in yours have I found
a sane point of view.

234
00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.100
- What do you mean, "sane"?
- Cigarette?

235
00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.800
- Hmm? Yeah. Thanks.
- You do not excuse what needs no excuse.

236
00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.600
Let me see.
How did you put it?

237
00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:34.600
"Hunting is as much a game
as stud poker...

238
00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:36.300
only the limits are higher."

239
00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:39.000
You have put our case perfectly.

240
00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:41.500
Then you're a hunter yourself?

241
00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:42.900
We are kindred spirits.

242
00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:45.500
It is my one passion.

243
00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:48.800
He sleeps all day
and hunts all night.

244
00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.600
And what's more, Rainsford,
he'll have you doing the same thing.

245
00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:53.900
We'll have capital sport together,
I hope.

246
00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:55.500
Don't encourage him.

247
00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.900
He's had our two sailors so busy...

248
00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.600
chasing around the woods
after flora and fauna...

249
00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:02.900
that we haven't
seen them for three days.

250
00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:07.000
But what do you hunt here?

251
00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.200
I'll tell you.

252
00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:12.800
You will be amused, I know.

253
00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.400
I have done a rare thing.

254
00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:18.200
I have invented
a new sensation.

255
00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:20.600
Yeah, and is he stingy with it.

256
00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:22.400
What is this sensation, Count?

257
00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:23.900
Mr. Rainsford,

258
00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.200
God made some men poets.

259
00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:29.300
Some He made kings,
some beggars.

260
00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:31.800
Me, He made a hunter.

261
00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.600
My hand was made for the trigger,
my father told me.

262
00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:41.200
He was a very rich man...

263
00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:44.700
with a quarter of a million acres
in the Crimea, and an ardent sportsman.

264
00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.400
When I was only still up high
he gave me my first gun.

265
00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:52.300
- Good for him.
- My life has been one glorious hunt.

266
00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:56.000
It would be impossible for me to tell
you how many animals I have killed.

267
00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:59.000
- But when the revolution...
- Look out.

268
00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:04.600
Oh, I'm so sorry.

269
00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:07.100
Count Zaroff
was so interesting...

270
00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:09.000
I didn't realize the danger.

271
00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:15.600
Oh, it's all right now. What were you
saying about the revolution, Count?

272
00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:19.200
Oh, merely that I escaped
with most of my fortune.

273
00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:23.400
Naturally, I continued to hunt
all over the world.

274
00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:28.000
It was in Africa that the Cape buffalo
gave me this.

275
00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:29.700
That must have been a close call.

276
00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.700
Yes. It still bothers me
sometimes.

277
00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:36.400
However, in two months
I was on my way to the Amazon.

278
00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:40.300
I'd heard that the jaguars there
were unusually cunning.

279
00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:44.000
No, no, no.
No sport at all.

280
00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:47.300
Well, conditions
are bad everywhere these days.

281
00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.000
One night, as I lay in my tent
with this...

282
00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.300
this head of mine...

283
00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:56.100
a terrible thought crept like a snake
into my brain.

284
00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.000
Hunting was beginning
to bore me.

285
00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:03.000
Is that such a terrible thought,
Count?

286
00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:07.800
It is, my dear lady, when hunting has
been the whip for all other passions.

287
00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:10.700
When I lost
my love of hunting...

288
00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:12.600
I lost my love of life...

289
00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:15.700
of love.

290
00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.000
Well, you seem to have
stood it pretty well.

291
00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:24.600
I even tried to sink myself
to the level of the savage.

292
00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:29.300
I made myself perfect in the use
of the Tartar war bow.

293
00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:31.600
Tartar which?

294
00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:33.800
Tartar war bow...

295
00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:35.600
That one up there.

296
00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:40.800
It's cute.

297
00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.700
Even to this day I prefer
to hunt with it...

298
00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:46.600
but alas,
even that was too deadly.

299
00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.400
What I needed
was not a new weapon...

300
00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:52.600
but a new animal.

301
00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:54.400
A new animal?

302
00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:56.600
Exactly so.

303
00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.300
You found one?

304
00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:01.400
Yes.

305
00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:05.600
Here on my island...

306
00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:10.000
I hunt
the most dangerous game.

307
00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:13.200
"The most dangerous game"?

308
00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:15.500
You mean tigers?

309
00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:17.400
Tigers?
No.

310
00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:22.600
The tiger has nothing
but his claws and his fangs.

311
00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:29.100
I heard some queer beast howling back
there along the water. Was that it?

312
00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:38.900
It's no use, Rainsford.

313
00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:40.200
He won't tell.

314
00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.700
He won't even let you see
his trophy room...

315
00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:47.800
till he gets ready to take you
on a hunt of the great whatsit.

316
00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:49.700
My one secret.

317
00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:52.900
I keep it as a surprise
for my guests...

318
00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:55.800
against the rainy day
of boredom.

319
00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:58.400
Listen old boy...
You let me in on that game...

320
00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:00.000
and I'll bet you
I go for it.

321
00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:03.100
You know, Rainsford,
he hasn't failed yet.

322
00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:06.100
If he says a thing is good,
it is good.

323
00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:09.300
He's a judge of liquor,
wizard at contract...

324
00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.900
plays the piano...
anything you want.

325
00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:13.800
He's a good host

326
00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:15.800
and a good scholar, eh, Count?

327
00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:16.900
Yes, yes.

328
00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:20.600
You want me to go hunting?
You just say the word. We're pals.

329
00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:23.300
We'll have a big party,
get cockeyed and go hunting.

330
00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:25.600
A completely civilized
point of view.

331
00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:27.000
I tell you what you do.

332
00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:30.100
You come to my place in
the Adirondacks, some time, see.

333
00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:34.300
We'll have a private car,
liquor and gals on the trip...

334
00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:37.100
and the guides
will make the deers behave.

335
00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:41.100
I think we'd better
change the subject.

336
00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:43.700
All right.
Change the subject.

337
00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:46.700
Oh, I know!
Play the piano, huh?

338
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:48.100
If you wish.

339
00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:52.800
Good idea. Play the piano.
Leave it to me, and I'll fix everything.

340
00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:55.400
Perhaps the count
doesn't want to play.

341
00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:59.600
There you go, sis,
throwing cold water.

342
00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:02.000
Leave me alone.
I know where the piano is.

343
00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:03.900
I'm perfectly sober.

344
00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:06.400
Charming simplicity.

345
00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:09.100
"Completely civilized,"
did you say?

346
00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:12.100
He talks of wine and women
as a prelude to the hunt.

347
00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:15.500
We barbarians know
that it is after the chase...

348
00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.100
and then only
that man revels.

349
00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.700
It does seem a bit
like cocktails before breakfast.

350
00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:23.500
Of course, yes. You know the saying
of the Ugandi chieftains...

351
00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:26.700
"Hunt first the enemy,
then the woman."

352
00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:28.700
That's the savages' idea
everywhere.

353
00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:30.400
It is the natural instinct.

354
00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:32.200
What is woman...

355
00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:34.700
even such a woman as this...

356
00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.700
until the blood is quickened
by the kill?

357
00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:41.400
- Oh, I don't know.
- "Oh, I don't know."

358
00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:43.900
You Americans.

359
00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:46.100
One passion builds upon another.

360
00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:48.200
Kill!

361
00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:50.300
Then love.

362
00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:52.600
When you have known that...

363
00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:54.700
you will have known ecstasy.

364
00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.100
Oh, Martin!

365
00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:05.300
Here you see Zaroff,
the keyboard king...

366
00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:07.500
in his Branca Island hour.

367
00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:09.500
Come on, Count.
Now, you show them.

368
00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.400
- What do you suggest?
- Oh, just a good tune.

369
00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:14.400
But not highbrow,
like last night.

370
00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:17.200
- Just a good tune, see?
- I see.

371
00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.700
Oh, his hunting dogs.

372
00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:47.300
Keep your voice low
and listen.

373
00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.600
It isn't true about the launch
needing repairs.

374
00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:53.000
I heard it leave the boathouse
last night. It returned this morning.

375
00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:55.300
You mean he's keeping you
from returning to the mainland?

376
00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:55.800
Yes.

377
00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.900
Well, perhaps he enjoys the company
of two very charming people.

378
00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.000
Two, maybe.
There were four of us a week ago.

379
00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:09.100
- The other two have disappeared.
- What do you mean?

380
00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:11.700
One night after dinner,
the count took one of our sailors...

381
00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:13.500
down to see his trophy room...

382
00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:16.400
at the foot
of those stone steps.

383
00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:19.700
- That iron door?
- Yes.

384
00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:22.200
Two nights later
he took the other there.

385
00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:25.200
Neither has been seen since.

386
00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:28.200
Have you asked him
about them?

387
00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:30.000
He says they've gone hunting.

388
00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:32.000
Oh, be careful.
He's watching us.

389
00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:35.400
Will you smile,
as if I'd said something funny?

390
00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:38.600
Now look here. You must be mistaken.

391
00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:39.400
Not now.

392
00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:41.800
Applaud.

393
00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:47.800
Attaboy! Attaboy!

394
00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:49.200
Thank you.

395
00:25:49.300 --> 00:25:52.000
What did I tell you?
Smacks a mean ivory, eh, Rainsford?

396
00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:54.200
It was splendid.
Don't stop, please.

397
00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:57.700
I'm afraid we have failed to hold
the full attention of our audience.

398
00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:01.500
Well, I expect it's rather difficult
for Mr. Rainsford...

399
00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:03.800
to concentrate on anything
after all he's been through.

400
00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:06.500
My dear lady,
you are pleading for yourself.

401
00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:10.300
I can see the drooping
of those lovely eyes.

402
00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:11.900
Excuse me.

403
00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:16.700
You know, the count's worse
than a family governess.

404
00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.200
Every night he sends us off to bed
like naughty children.

405
00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:21.500
Oh, no, my dear. No.

406
00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:24.500
Charming children.

407
00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:29.500
There, you hear that, sis?

408
00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.900
Now trot along upstairs
and don't bother us grownups anymore.

409
00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:36.200
Well, after that I guess...
I guess I'll have to go.

410
00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.500
- Good night, Mr. Rainsford.
- Good night.

411
00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:40.300
We'll be seeing each other
at breakfast.

412
00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:42.500
- Good night.
- Good night.

413
00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:45.800
Good night, sis. We won't
be seeing each other at breakfast.

414
00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:53.000
Oh, my dear Rainsford,
I have been most inconsiderate.

415
00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:55.600
You must be feeling
the need of sleep too.

416
00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:59.200
- Yes, I am just about all in.
- Then Ivan will show you to your room.

417
00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:08.100
Oh, Martin,
turn in early, please?

418
00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:12.200
Don't worry. The count'll
take care of me, all right.

419
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:15.200
Indeed I shall.

420
00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:34.300
- Well, good night.
- Good night, sir.

421
00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:35.800
Sleep well.

422
00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:50.100
Oh, uh, well, here's to long life.

423
00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:51.600
A long life.

424
00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:55.500
Tell me, Mr. Trowbridge...

425
00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.000
are you also fatigued?

426
00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:00.100
Tired? Me?

427
00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:01.500
You know I'm not.

428
00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:04.600
You know, Rainsford,
we two are just alike.

429
00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.400
Up all night and sleep all day.

430
00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:09.300
Well, good night.

431
00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:13.800
Well, what are we
gonna do, huh?

432
00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:19.800
What's the big idea?

433
00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:22.400
I thought that perhaps...

434
00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:24.100
tonight

435
00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:28.300
you would like to
see my trophy room?

436
00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:30.100
Your trophy room?

437
00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:33.100
I'm sure
you will find it most...

438
00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:36.300
interesting.

439
00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:38.700
Say, that's a great idea.

440
00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:42.500
Ho-ho. Now we're pals.
No more secrets now, huh?

441
00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:46.100
- We'll make a night of it.
- I hope so, Mr. Trowbridge.

442
00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:49.500
Just you and I... pals.
We'll have fun together, huh?

443
00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:51.400
Precisely, yes.

444
00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:54.300
Fun together.

445
00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:58.100
Attaboy, County, old boy,
old boy, County.

446
00:29:37.900 --> 00:29:39.300
Please let me come in.

447
00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.300
I'm sorry to disturb you,
but I'm frightened.

448
00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:45.200
- What was it? Those dogs?
- My brother.

449
00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:47.600
I've been listening for hours
for him to come upstairs.

450
00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:50.400
I've just gone to his room.
He isn't there!

451
00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:53.700
- He's probably somewhere with the count.
- That's just what I'm afraid of.

452
00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:57.700
Count Zaroff is planning something...
about my brother and me.

453
00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:00.600
You don't really think anything
has happened to your brother?

454
00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:02.400
Oh, I don't know,
but we've got to find him.

455
00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:03.900
Won't you help me?

456
00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:06.500
Why, of course I'll help you.

457
00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:10.100
- Where do you think he's gone?
- Where did the others go?

458
00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:14.400
The iron door.

459
00:30:15.800 --> 00:30:17.500
I'll meet you downstairs
in five minutes.

460
00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:19.000
Thank you.

461
00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:50.400
That's queer.
It's unlocked.

462
00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:48.500
Zaroff! He's coming down.

463
00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.200
Back here, quick!

464
00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:28.200
Where is my brother?

465
00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:29.500
Where is my brother?

466
00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:47.300
You killed him!

467
00:32:49.300 --> 00:32:51.500
You killed my brother!

468
00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:53.600
You!

469
00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:55.000
Why, you...

470
00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:17.500
Come, come, my dear Rainsford.

471
00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:20.100
I don't want to treat you
like my other guests.

472
00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:22.900
You and I,
we are hunters.

473
00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:27.700
So that's
your most dangerous game.

474
00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:31.300
Yes. My dear fellow,
I intended to tell you last night...

475
00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:33.200
but you know, Miss Trowbridge...

476
00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:34.500
You hunted him like an animal.

477
00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:36.600
I know what you think,
but you are wrong.

478
00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:39.500
He was sober and fit for sport
when I sent him out.

479
00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:43.200
An hour or two strapped up in here
brought him to his senses.

480
00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:44.500
You raving maniac!

481
00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:49.300
Yes, yes, yes. I'll take it off...
when we've finished.

482
00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:53.000
The stupid fellow tried to escape
through the swamps of Fog Hollow.

483
00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:56.600
You see, when I first began
stocking my island...

484
00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:59.000
many of my guests
thought I was joking...

485
00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:01.500
so I established
this trophy room.

486
00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:04.800
I always bring them here
before the hunt.

487
00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:07.700
An hour with my trophies...

488
00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:10.800
and they usually do their best
to keep away from me.

489
00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.000
Where do you get
these poor devils?

490
00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:17.900
Providence provided my island
with dangerous reefs.

491
00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:21.300
But there are light buoys
to mark the safe channel.

492
00:34:22.900 --> 00:34:25.200
They do not always mark it.

493
00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:28.300
- You shifted them.
- Precisely right.

494
00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:30.700
Too bad your yacht
should have suffered...

495
00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:33.100
but at least
it brought us together.

496
00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:35.700
You take half-drowned men
from ships you've wrecked...

497
00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:37.800
and drive them out
to be hunted.

498
00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:40.100
I give them
every consideration...

499
00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:42.400
good food, exercise...

500
00:34:42.700 --> 00:34:45.100
everything to get them
in splendid shape.

501
00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:47.800
- To be shot down in cold blood.
- Oh, no, no.

502
00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:50.800
Oh, I admit,
with this annoying fellow...

503
00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:54.200
but usually I give them
hunting clothes...

504
00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:57.300
a woodsman's knife
and a full day's start.

505
00:34:57.600 --> 00:35:01.400
I even wait until midnight to give them
the full advantage the dark.

506
00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:04.400
And if one eludes me
only till sunrise...

507
00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:05.900
he wins the game.

508
00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:10.100
Suppose he refuses
to be hunted.

509
00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:15.300
Ivan is such an artist
with these.

510
00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:21.600
Invariably, Mr. Rainsford,
invariably they choose to hunt.

511
00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:23.700
And when they win?

512
00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:31.200
To date I have not lost.

513
00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:35.400
Oh, Rainsford, you'll find
this game worth playing.

514
00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:39.300
When the next ship arrives,
we'll have gorgeous sport together.

515
00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:43.000
You murdering rat!
I'm a hunter not an assassin.

516
00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:46.000
Come, Rainsford.
Say you will hunt with me.

517
00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:49.100
Hunt men?

518
00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:52.200
Say you will hunt with me!

519
00:35:58.600 --> 00:35:59.600
No?

520
00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.200
What do you think I am?

521
00:36:03.700 --> 00:36:06.800
One, I fear, who dare not
follow his own convictions...

522
00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:08.600
to their logical conclusion.

523
00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:12.900
I'm afraid in this instance,
Mr. Rainsford...

524
00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:15.700
you may have to follow them.

525
00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:19.300
What do you mean?

526
00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:22.500
I shall not wait
for the next ship.

527
00:36:24.600 --> 00:36:27.100
Four o'clock.
The sun is just rising.

528
00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.500
Come, Mr. Rainsford.
Let us not waste time.

529
00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:50.900
Ivan.

530
00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.300
Your fangs and claws,
Mr. Rainsford.

531
00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:02.500
Bob! Bob!

532
00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:16.300
Ivan.

533
00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:19.900
Bob! Bob, what are they
going to do?

534
00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:22.900
- I'm going to be hunted.
- Oh, no. No, Miss Trowbridge.

535
00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:24.100
Outdoor chess.

536
00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:26.400
His brain against mine.

537
00:37:26.900 --> 00:37:28.800
His woodcraft against mine.

538
00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:32.000
And the prize?

539
00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:34.300
The prize?

540
00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:36.600
You may recall
what I said last evening.

541
00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:39.800
Only after the kill...

542
00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:43.500
does man know
the true ecstasy of love.

543
00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:46.700
Suppose you lose.

544
00:37:48.200 --> 00:37:49.300
If I do not...

545
00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:52.300
What shall I say?...
find you...

546
00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:57.900
between midnight and sunrise tomorrow,
freedom for both of you.

547
00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:01.500
- I'm going with you.
- No. He'll kill you too.

548
00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:04.200
Not at all.
One does not kill a female animal.

549
00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:07.700
If you lose,
I can easily recapture her alive.

550
00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:10.200
All right.
I'll take her with me then.

551
00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:12.400
We'll set him a trail
he'll remember.

552
00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:20.800
It's only fair to advise you
against Fog Hollow.

553
00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:25.900
Outdoor chess, Mr. Rainsford.

554
00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:43.300
Don't lose your nerve.
We'll beat this thing.

555
00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:45.700
- The others didn't.
- We will.

556
00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:57.200
Come on.
Let's get going.

557
00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:28.000
I feel as though
we've come miles.

558
00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:29.200
Yeah,

559
00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:32.300
but three hours doesn't take
you far in this jungle.

560
00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:34.100
Come on.
Let's keep going.

561
00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:35.400
Come on.

562
00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:47.100
Just a little more of this,
then easy downhill going.

563
00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:48.800
We'll soon be safe.

564
00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:15.400
No wonder he was so sure.

565
00:40:15.900 --> 00:40:18.200
This island is no bigger
than a deer park.

566
00:40:18.700 --> 00:40:20.000
Oh, Bob!

567
00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:23.100
Come on, now.

568
00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:26.300
What are we going to do?

569
00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:30.700
We didn't each live through a shipwreck
to let this crazy man hunter worry us.

570
00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:35.000
I shouldn't have come with you.
You might beat him if you were alone.

571
00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:38.000
Alone? And leave you here
with that savage?

572
00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:40.200
Not a chance.

573
00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:44.800
Now we've got to think
of something to worry him.

574
00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:47.800
You'd never get near him.
He'd shoot on sight.

575
00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:50.400
Weapons aren't everything
in the jungle.

576
00:40:51.400 --> 00:40:54.100
Say, did you notice
that leaning tree down there?

577
00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:58.300
- The one we just passed?
- Yes. I want to show you something.

578
00:41:02.800 --> 00:41:05.600
You see? If that supporting branch
were cut away...

579
00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:08.000
this fallen tree would make a perfect
Malay deadfall.

580
00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:09.700
A Malay deadfall? What's that?

581
00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:11.900
A man-killing contraption
the natives use.

582
00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:14.600
It would stop that madman,
all right.

583
00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:16.900
Trouble is, it takes
quite a few hours to build.

584
00:41:17.400 --> 00:41:19.400
He said he wouldn't follow
till midnight.

585
00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:22.000
That's right. If you help me,
I think we'll have time.

586
00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.300
Come on.
We'll cut some strong vines.

587
00:41:28.700 --> 00:41:30.500
There. Almost ready.

588
00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:34.300
This bracelet of yours makes
a fine guide ring for my necktie.

589
00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:37.100
He'll have been on his way
almost an hour now.

590
00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:39.900
Look out!
Don't touch that trip line.

591
00:41:40.100 --> 00:41:41.900
You'll have a two-ton tree
down on your back.

592
00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:45.100
- Jungle wood's as heavy as iron.
- Will it really work?

593
00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:47.800
I've never known a living thing
to get by one yet.

594
00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:50.600
Look here.
You touch that trip line...

595
00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:53.400
it'll pull that trigger free.

596
00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:57.200
Once that's loose, there's nothing
to keep the log from coming down.

597
00:41:57.500 --> 00:41:59.400
It'll crash down
and kill anything underneath it.

598
00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:04.500
Look.

599
00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:12.300
We're ready.
Let him come.

600
00:42:30.100 --> 00:42:31.400
Give me that knife.

601
00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:34.800
Come out, Rainsford.

602
00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:36.700
Why prolong it?

603
00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.000
I'll not bungle this shot.

604
00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:41.000
You'll never even feel it.

605
00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:46.100
But surely you don't think
that anyone who has hunted leopards...

606
00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:48.400
would follow you
into that ambush?

607
00:43:55.300 --> 00:43:58.300
Oh, very well.
If you choose to play the leopard...

608
00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:01.400
I shall hunt you
like a leopard.

609
00:44:20.900 --> 00:44:22.800
Wait. Maybe it's a trick.

610
00:44:47.300 --> 00:44:48.200
Eve.

611
00:44:59.100 --> 00:45:00.700
Why did he go?

612
00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:03.500
He's playing with us...
like a cat with a mouse.

613
00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:05.000
What do you mean?

614
00:45:05.300 --> 00:45:07.400
You heard him say he'd hunt us
as he'd hunt a leopard.

615
00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:09.800
That means he's gone
for his high-powered rifle.

616
00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:12.400
His rifle?
Oh, Bob, we must get away from here!

617
00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:14.200
- Run, quick!
- Eve, wait.

618
00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:38.400
- No, I tell you, no!
- Don't stop! No!

619
00:45:38.800 --> 00:45:41.400
Wait. That's Fog Hollow ahead.

620
00:45:42.100 --> 00:45:45.100
- Fog Hollow?
- The swamp where he caught the others.

621
00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:47.500
We haven't a chance
of keeping ahead of him there.

622
00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:50.200
- But there's no place else to run.
- That's just what he's counting on.

623
00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:54.100
We've got two hours till dawn. We've got
to use our brains instead of our legs.

624
00:45:54.200 --> 00:45:55.500
But he'll have his rifle.

625
00:45:56.000 --> 00:45:57.800
And we'll have a man trap.
Look.

626
00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:01.800
It makes me dizzy.

627
00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:11.000
Cover this over.

628
00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:15.400
When Mr. Zaroff falls down there,
he'll be all through hunting.

629
00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:18.400
Quick. Gather some leaves and grass.
I'll cut some branches.

630
00:48:06.900 --> 00:48:08.700
Yes. Very good, Rainsford.

631
00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:10.500
Very good.

632
00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:13.100
You have not won yet.

633
00:48:14.500 --> 00:48:16.100
Look at your watch.

634
00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:21.000
Are you looking at it?

635
00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:23.800
Still half an hour till sunrise.

636
00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:26.200
Swamp or no swamp,
we can keep ahead of him that long.

637
00:48:27.700 --> 00:48:30.800
As you are doubtless saying,
the odds are against me.

638
00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:35.500
You have made my rifle useless
in the fog.

639
00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:39.400
You cannot blame me
if I overcome that obstacle.

640
00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:53.800
Those animals I cornered...
now I know how they felt.

641
00:56:52.700 --> 00:56:53.800
Achmed,

642
00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:56.400
Miss Trowbridge...

643
00:56:57.000 --> 00:56:58.400
bring her here.

644
00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:01.400
Now!

645
00:57:57.100 --> 00:58:00.000
My dear Rainsford,
I congratulate you.

646
00:58:08.100 --> 00:58:09.800
You have beaten me.

647
00:58:12.400 --> 00:58:13.600
Not yet.

648
00:58:14.200 --> 00:58:16.300
Oh, but of course.
I insist.

649
00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:24.300
Why, you're...

650
00:58:25.500 --> 00:58:27.400
You're not even wounded.

651
00:58:28.200 --> 00:58:29.700
You hit the dog not me.

652
00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:31.700
I took a chance
and went over with him.

653
00:58:34.300 --> 00:58:36.100
A clever trick, Rainsford.

654
00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:38.800
I cheerfully admit defeat.

655
00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:44.200
Here's the key
of the boathouse.

656
00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:47.900
The door is
in the trophy room.

657
00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:51.200
You and Miss Trowbridge
may leave at once.

658
00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:51.900
No!

659
01:00:23.200 --> 01:00:24.200
Bob!

660
01:00:32.700 --> 01:00:33.600
Eve!

661
01:00:36.000 --> 01:00:37.000
The boat! Quick!

662
01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:36.800
Impossible!
